\par\par \b \uc2 Indian cash rates fall; RBI  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2  eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2012\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 24 (Reuters) - Indian overnight cash rates fell on Thursday as demand waned from banks which had already borrowed heavily over the last few sessions, traders said.\par \par \uc2 However, liquidity continues to stay tight given the lack of fresh debt redemptions or interest payments, which is likely to keep the cash rate above the repo rate of 8 percent in the near-term, say traders.\par \par \uc2 Expectations the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as the rupee continues to hit record lows are also expected to keep rupee liquidity tight.\par \par \uc2 "People had borrowed more than their requirement over the last 4 to 5 days and hence the call market volumes have also come down," said Debendra Dash, a fixed income dealer at \uc2 Development Credit Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Further outlook on liquidity really depends on the how much the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he added.\par \par \uc2 The one-day money closed at 8.15/20 percent versus previous close of 8.25/30 percent.\par \par \uc2 Liquidity remains tight, with banks' borrowing from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repo window at 958.25 billion rupees on Thursday, reflecting the extent of cash tightness.\par \par \uc2 Still, some investors have been comforted as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up bond purchases via open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to offset its dollar sales in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Volume in the call money market was sharply lower at 146.64 billion rupees, compared with 193.84 billion rupees on Wednesday and 210.50 billion rupees on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The weighted average rate was slightly lower at 8.24 percent from 8.26 percent on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Volume in the collateralised borrowing and lending obligation (CBLO) market rose slightly to 426 billion rupees versus 410.08 billion rupees on Wednesday, with the weighted average rate 3 basis points higher at 7.99 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the inter-bank repo market, volume was 154.59 billion rupees at a weighted average rate of 8.08 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Rafael Nam)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA/CALL | ABN | D | M | SNI | SNS | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120524e85o000sz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
