\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: India Not Looking At Direct Dollar Sales To Oil Firms - Official\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2012\par 13:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Comments likely to put more pressure on rupee\par \par \uc2 -- Oil companies account for 10% of dollar deals, with $2 billion-$3 billion weekly demand\par \par \uc2 -- Official says oil companies have enough money, and crude prices falling\par \par \uc2 (Rewrites throughout, adds finance ministry official's comments, updates \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  level, adds context and background all through)\par \par \uc2 By Prasanta Sahu and Sudeep Jain\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--The Indian government Wednesday dampened market expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would sell dollars directly to oil importers at a fixed daily rate, with a senior finance ministry official saying there're no such plans to support the battered rupee.\par \par \uc2 The comment could hurt the rupee because oil importers -- which account for about one of every $10 transacted in the market -- are a significant reason for the recent surge in demand for the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts believe that the direct selling of the greenback to oil importers -- a mechanism referred to as a "dollar window" -- would isolate a key source of demand from the broader market.\par \par \uc2 This would ease pressure on the rupee as India's oil companies alone buy $2 billion-$3 billion of the greenback every week.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry official's comments to reporters were out of sync with a statement earlier Wednesday by C. Rangarajan, chairman of the influential Prime Minister's Economic Advisory Council. Rangarajan had said that selling dollars directly to oil companies could be an option to limit the rupee's fall.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee has been in a furious downward spiral over the past week, with the dollar punching through key barriers every day to continue notching up all-time highs.\par \par \uc2 Since the middle of March, the local unit has fallen more than 10% and is now more than 22% lower than a year earlier. At 1108 GMT Wednesday, the dollar was trading at INR56.08 after touching a record high of INR56.22.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have been in the market heavily in recent days -- including on Wednesday -- but with little effect.\par \par \uc2 While a weak rupee helps India's exporters by making their products and services cheaper, it makes imports -- especially that of oil -- more expensive and drives up already high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's latest slide began after the federal budget in March shook investor confidence and led to a large outflow of cash.\par \par \uc2 The budget unveiled proposals which many believe are detrimental to foreign investor sentiment, including tax proposals giving authorities sweeping powers to examine deals they feel are structured solely to avoid taxes.\par \par \uc2 The pain is aggravated by India's yawning fiscal and current account deficits.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's fall picked up pace recently, with the dollar rising against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a flight to safety sparked by the possibility of Greece leaving the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Asian markets and commodities fell across the region Wednesday after a former Greek prime minister said preparations for an exit from the euro zone are being considered.\par \par \uc2 The comments sent investors to the U.S. dollar in overnight trading, with the euro stabilizing in Asia at $1.2669.\par \par \uc2 The rush to the greenback symbolized a diminishing risk appetite, which has pushed down commodity prices as well.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in India are refusing to stick their necks out and put a bottom to the rupee.\par \par \uc2 But they agree that a dollar window for oil companies would help.\par \par \uc2 "The impact would depend on how many dollars they [the RBI] provide, but it would be much more effective than direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Vivek Rajpal, a strategist with Nomura \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the finance ministry official who said there are no plans for direct sales added that the oil companies have enough cash at the moment, and that global crude prices are falling anyway.\par \par \uc2 He said also that reforms on foreign direct investment in multi-brand retail and civil aviation are expected soon, which will bring in overseas funds and provide support to the rupee.\par \par \uc2 --By Sudeep Jain and Prasanta Sahu, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6123; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 23, 2012 07:24 ET (11:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120523e85n003vc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
