\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Emerging-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Suffer As Euro-zone Fears Build\b0\par\par\uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2012\par 18:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Forint, rupee, real among biggest fallers as safety of dollars sought\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by emerging-market policy makers so far deemed inadequate\par \par \uc2 -- Market nerves likely to persist, sparking some speculation about a possible emerging-market crisis \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laura Clarke \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under the cosh Wednesday as investors lost confidence in the ability of policy makers to resolve euro-zone tensions and support flagging growth, prompting a flight of capital out of less liquid markets and into the safety of the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian rupee was a standout decliner as the dollar hit an all-time record high above INR56.20. The Korean won fell to a five-month low and the Brazilian real hit its worst level since May 2009 as worries about the ramifications of a possible Greek exit from the euro zone intensified, with Greek elections increasingly seen as a referendum on the issue due June 17.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, the Hungarian forint sank to its weakest level in about four months against even the unloved euro, which rose to as high as HUF303.35, almost 6% higher than it was at the start of the month. The Polish zloty also hit a four-month low against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is now a massive global markets issue," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  emerging-market analyst Benoit Anne, ahead of an informal gathering of European leaders to discuss measures to boost growth and reassure frazzled markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  typically are a sensitive barometer of broader market sentiment. They tend to fare better than major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when optimism is high but worse when markets are running scared as global investors cancel non-core bets, repatriate capital and seek out the safety of the dollar or yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Efforts by local central banks to staunch the sell-off have so far offered little relief. In the case of Brazil, the weaker real has also been welcomed as a boon to exporters even as the pace of depreciation has threatened to become disorderly, creating some policy confusion, while suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the rupee has been inadequate and barely made a dent, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are guaranteed more weeks of severe stress and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can't be patchy; authorities must show their teeth," Anne said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, accusations the Hungarian government has been dragging its feet continued to dog the forint even as prospects improved Wednesday for a financing agreement between it, the European Union and International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The forint is finally paying the price for the lack of clarity on financing. What level does the forint have to go to for the government to get really serious about cutting a deal with the IMF?" asked Timothy Ash, head of emerging-markets research at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, with the euro-zone crisis threatening to spill out of control and the global economy in a fragile state as Chinese growth slows and the U.S. recovery stutters, governments from emerging markets could be left powerless to contain further weakness in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to some market observers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Stephen Jen of SLJ Macro Partners said Monday: "Though I'm unsure if this is indeed the beginning of an emerging-markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis, I think there is a high likelihood that such a crisis will take place...this year."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1506 GMT the Hungarian forint traded at HUF303.25 and HUF240.75 against the euro and dollar respectively. Against the dollar, the Indian rupee was at INR56.01, the Brazilian real was at BRL2.0915 and the Korean won was at 1,175.3.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Laura Clarke, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9496; laura.clarke@dowjones.com; @djfxtrader\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (William Kemble-Diaz and Clare Connaghan contributed to this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 23, 2012 12:22 ET (16:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120523e85n00627\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
