\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Indian rupee skids to new low; more falls seen\b0\par\par\uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2012\par 10:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee hits record low; more falls seen ahead\par \par \uc2 * Policymakers lack of response seen worsening falls\par \par \uc2 * RBI hands seen tied; oil dollar sales would help\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 23 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee slumped to another record low against the dollar on Wednesday, ratcheting up pressure on policymakers to restore confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when appetite for risky assets is ebbing around the world.\par \par \uc2 After the markets closed, the government announced that it will allow state-run companies to raise gasoline prices, a measure that on its own will have negligible fiscal impact but that is at least seen as a signal that India could begin to look at fuel reform - a key concern for investors.\par \par \uc2 But without government action, analysts saw no near-term catalysts to revive the rupee, which on Wednesday fell beyond the symbolically significant level of 56 to hit a record low of 56.225 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Traders said it could weaken further to 57, extending a drop of more than 13 percent from its yearly peak in February.\par \par \uc2 "This step is just a flash in the pan and does not change the fiscal figures," said Indranil Pan, chief economist at \uc2 Kotak Mahindra Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "So I don't see any positive impact on rupee from this development. Only if diesel, LPG, kerosene prices are increased it will have implications on the fiscal figures,"\par \par \uc2 Global risk aversion due to the crisis in the euro zone has exposed India's vulnerabilities, most prominently a current account deficit that reached 4.3 percent of GDP in the December quarter. The economy depends on capital flows to bridge the gap.\par \par \uc2 Worries about the government's ability to fix its finances have also weighed heavily on the rupee, as investors worry the government will overshoot its deficit target of 5.1 percent of gross domestic product for the fiscal year ending in March 2013.\par \par \uc2 Easing the government's fuel subsidy burden has been seen as a key step, despite the potential inflationary impact.\par \par \uc2 Other factors that have made the rupee Asia's weakest-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year have included sluggish policy reforms and a recent controversial tax proposal on foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 "India is one of those odd cases where the bottom-up corporate environment is very strong, but it is challenged by poor politics and macroeconomic policies," said Pranay Gupta, Asia chief investment officer for Lombard Odier.\par \par \uc2 The political will to take tough steps to put Asia's third largest economy back on a firm growth path was lacking, he said.\par \par \uc2 'TIME TO PANIC'?\par \par \uc2 Wednesday marked the sixth consecutive session in which the rupee has hit a record low against the dollar. It recovered slightly to close the day at 55.9950/56.0050.\par \par \uc2 Non-deliverable forwards are pricing in more weakness, implying levels of 56.46 in a month's time and 57.25 in three months. Citing technical chart analysis, Standard Chartered said in a report on Tuesday the rupee could fall to as low as 58.62.\par \par \uc2 Given the absence of government measures, traders have focused on steps the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take.\par \par \uc2 However, after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has turned cautious on selling dollars because global risk aversion is undermining most emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders estimated the RBI sold $100-$250 million earlier on Wednesday in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in three sessions. But the amount was too small to stem the rupee's fall.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken several measures to try to stem the rupee's slide, including raising deposit rates for non-resident Indians and forcing exporters to convert half of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings into rupees, but to no major effect.\par \par \uc2 The RBI could do more, such as selling dollars to state oil importers, which Nomura argues would reduce dollar demand in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by $8.8 billion per month.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen mindful that its foreign reserves total nearly $292 billion, enough for roughly six months of imports, down from $320 billion in October.\par \par \uc2 Instead, analysts say that the best defense for the rupee rests with the Indian government, which they say needs to implement more confidence-boosting measures about its finances and stimulate investment.\par \par \uc2 "It is the time to press the panic button," said Rupa Rege Nitsure, chief economist at \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The government has to do something right now to reverse investor sentiment, so that India can start attracting capital inflows," she added.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  downgraded India's economic growth forecast for 2012 to 6.3 percent this week, below the levels of the Lehman crisis, citing the government's propensity for fiscal policies that support consumption at the expense of private investment.\par \par \uc2 At least for now, foreign investors have not yet staged an exodus from Indian markets. Though they sold a net $930 million in debt and stocks in April, th ey are slight buyers in May as of Tuesday and significant net buyers in the year to date.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Subhadip Sircar and Charmian Kok; Writing by Rafael Nam; Editing by Tony Munroe and Sanjeev Miglani)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA/RUPEE (UPDATE 3) | ABN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | D | E | RBN | M | O | OIL | ABX | BNX | FUN | SNI | SNS | RNP | DNP | PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120526e85n0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
