\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Nosedives To New All-Time Low; Bonds Gain\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2012\par 15:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     At 1200 GMT \par                          Latest      Change \par    USD/INR                56.00      +0.61 \par    8.79% 2021 Bond        8.51%      -1 BPs \par    Call Rate              8.26%      +2 BPs \par    Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    April                  2.84       -0.02 \par     *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount are midpoints of bid-offer spreads \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee nosedived past a key psychological level to a new all-time low against the U.S. dollar Wednesday as poor risk sentiment rattled investors region-wide.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at INR56.00 late Wednesday in Asia, compared with INR55.39 late Tuesday. It rose to INR56.22 earlier in the day, breaching the key INR56.00 level and topping its previous record high for a sixth straight session.\par \par \uc2 The euro sank to its lowest level against the dollar since August 2010. Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , too, were held hostage to skepticism that European leaders meeting in Brussels would announce more measures to palliate the euro-zone crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at least twice Wednesday to stem the rupee's fall. Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to underperform peers, such as the Philippine peso which slid to a five-month low against the greenback, or the South Korean won.\par \par \uc2 The rupee, vulnerable to weak fundamentals, is being exploited to the fullest by the global risk aversion, Alpari Financial Services said in a note.\par \par \uc2 India struggles with a gaping twin deficits on the budget and current account, while capital inflows are relatively weak.\par \par \uc2 "Unless the government initiates major policy action to cut its spending, or international crude prices decline sharply, India will remain exposed to any potential slowdown in capital inflows," \uc2 Chetan Ahya\uc2 , economist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , said in a note.\par \par \uc2 This would increase the pressure on the exchange rate, he added.\par \par \uc2 In the government debt market, bonds ended higher on flight to safer assets. Also, traders found relief that the current 10-year benchmark bond will continue to be liquid as the government doesn't plan replace it with a new note.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 8.79% 2021 bond ended at INR101.79, up from INR101.72 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, a senior finance ministry official told Dow Jones Newswires that the government will continue borrowing through other existing longer-term bonds, but won't issue a new 10-year bond.\par \par \uc2 Traders now look forward to a bond buyback by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, worth INR120 billion, to ease liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a note that continuation of these open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed to sustain lower bond yields.\par \par \uc2 "This is likely to provide the market with some comfort that the RBI will continue to offset incremental liquidity tightness and aim to prevent the call [rate] fixing from moving sharply higher from current levels," it said.\par \par \uc2 -By Khushita Vasant, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6122; khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 23-05-12 1311GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120523e85n000vk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
