\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Continues Plunge; Now At 55.34 Against US Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2012\par 11:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee fell at a frantic pace Tuesday, continuing to find new all-time lows as risk sentiment took a beating after Fitch cut Japan's sovereign rating earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 The rupee slid to 55.39 against the U.S. dollar, compared with 55.03 at Monday's close. The greenback was last quoting at INR55.34.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said there was no sign of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to break the rupee's fall.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a stated policy of getting into the market only to control volatility. Earlier Tuesday, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said that the RBI will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if and when needed.\par \par \uc2 Indian stocks extended their losses as well -- following weakness in riskier assets across the region -- with the \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's main benchmark ending down a provisional 0.9%.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has fallen to fresh record lows in all of the past five sessions.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's plunge comes just a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced more steps to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation.\par \par \uc2 In a notification late Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sealed the window for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers to play the futures and forwards markets off each other by imposing restrictions limiting the volatility of the rupee.\par \par \uc2 The measures helped the rupee start trading higher Tuesday, but steady dollar buying by oil importers soon began to hurt.\par \par \uc2 India is caught in a vicious cycle of rising fuel import costs because of a weak rupee, which swells its fiscal and current account deficits.\par \par \uc2 This in turn hurts investor confidence and prompts outflows during times of global risk aversion, further denting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also, while foreign fund inflows have taken a beating of late because of falling investor confidence in India, overseas reasons are to blame for the rupee's slide as well.\par \par \uc2 Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in Hong Kong, warned that the rupee is at the mercy of the external environment and may fall to 57 against the greenback before elections in Greece are held in June.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is highly unlikely to return to below 50 in the medium-term, he said in a note Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Tuesday downgraded Japan's sovereign rating to A+ and said it is maintaining a negative outlook due to the "leisurely" pace of the country's efforts to fix its fiscal situation.\par \par \uc2 --By Khushita Vasant, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6122; khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 22-05-12 1010GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020120522e85m0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
