\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: India's Rupee Freefall Hurts Stock Investors\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shefali Anand, Khushita Vasant and Raghavendra Upadhyaya   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2012\par 13:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued a freefall Tuesday, propelled to new lows over concerns about the country's economic outlook--a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decline which is exacerbating losses for foreign investors in India's stock and bond markets.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee fell to a fresh record low Tuesday of 55.47 rupees for one U.S. dollar, making it one of the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia this year.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost around 12% against the U.S. dollar over the last three months and is 23% lower than a year ago due to a host of economic problems facing India, including high fiscal and current account deficits, slowing economic growth and government paralysis on business reforms. The rupee has fallen to fresh record lows in its past five trading sessions.\par \par \uc2 Indian stocks also have been losing value, leading to a double-whammy for foreign investors. India's benchmark \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's Sensitive Index is down 13% over the past three months.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Funds that measure their returns in U.S. dollars have been even more heavily hit. India-focused mutual funds available to U.S. investors have lost 18% on average in the past three months, according to research firm \uc2 Morningstar Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds, too, have been hit, although they have been able to limit losses through buying derivative products that spread their risk. The average India-focused hedge fund has lost 5% over three months through end-April, compared to a 1.7% average gain for Asia funds, excluding Japan, in the same period, said Singapore-based research firm Eurekahedge Pte.\par \par \uc2 "Regardless of their portfolio performance, if the rupee depreciates then they will take a hit," said Farhan Mumtaz, an analyst at Eurekahedge.\par \par \uc2 While Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have lost some value against the dollar in recent days as globally risk-averse investors have loaded up on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , India is faring worse because of deepening concerns about its macro-economic stability.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's losses stem from India's widening current account deficit and lack of robust capital inflows amid high inflation and failure to push through much-needed economic reforms. In recent days, global risk aversion caused by uncertainty over the euro-zone debt crisis has added to pressures on the rupee.\par \par \uc2 For now, foreign investors are generally holding on to their Indian investments, hoping for a rebound. But prolonged local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness could at some point force investors to cut their losses, further weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Foreign institutional investors have pulled out $163 million from Indian stocks since April, reversing their optimism during the first three months of the year when they bought Indian stocks worth $8.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are dour on the outlook for the rupee in the near term, particularly because India has taken few steps to correct its fiscal and economic imbalances such as reducing massive fuel subsidies or allowing more foreign investment in India's retail sector.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, recent measures by India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at bolstering the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as forcing exporters to convert their dollar reserves into rupees, have failed to lift the rupee. The bank has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but without success. There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, traders said.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But many analysts believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has foreign reserves equal to only seven months' worth of imports, may have exhausted its firepower to support the rupee.\par \par \uc2 The most pessimistic forecasts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go as low as 60 rupees per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "It seems that policy makers lack a coherent plan to stabilize the INR in the short term or to boost growth," wrote Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB, in a research note Monday.\par \par \uc2 While money managers who invest in Indian stocks have been prepared for some volatility in the rupee, the losses have been larger and faster than most had expected.\par \par \uc2 "This month has been painful," said Samir Arora, a hedge fund manager at Helios Capital Management Pte., based in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Some money managers have begun to cut back on their allocations for Indian stocks in regional portfolios in recent months.\par \par \uc2 "In our portfolios, we are currently finding more opportunities in China and Korea," said Andrew Beal, an Asia-focused fund manager at U.K.'s \uc2 Henderson Global Investors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said that the falling rupee is a symptom of the deeper structural malaise in the Indian economy. "The situation may need to get worse before a coalition of pro-reform groups can push ahead with desperately needed measures to open and up and deregulate the economy," Beal said.\par \par \uc2 In general, foreign money managers are bracing themselves for a tough couple of years ahead for India.\par \par \uc2 "Many foreign investors are concerned about further deterioration in India's macro-economic condition in combination with policy and political uncertainty," said \uc2 Seth Freeman\uc2 , chief investment officer at San Francisco-based \uc2 EM Capital Management LLC\uc2 . "These investors are largely taking a wait and see strategy."\par \par \uc2 To be sure, some long-term India bulls are taking the rupee's losses in their stride and say its part of doing business in India.\par \par \uc2 "We think there's a possibility that it could get worse, but I don't think it should ring major alarm bells," said Sukumar Rajah, a Indian-based money manager with Franklin Templeton Investments of the U.S. "If the sentiment looks up, there's a chance that rupee would go up," he adds.\par \par \uc2 -By Shefali Anand, Khushita Vasant and Raghavendra Upadhyaya, The Wall Street Journal; Shefali.Anand@wsj.com [ 05-22-12 0748ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120522e85m0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
