\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: The Rupee Hasn't Hit Bottom Yet\b0\par\par\uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2012\par 08:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's India Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Shefali Anand\par \par \uc2 Having breached an all-time low, what's next for the Indian rupee?\par \par \uc2 On Thursday morning, the rupee bounced back from the all-time low of 54.52 rupees for one U.S. dollar it hit on Wednesday to trade at 54.31.\par \par \uc2 Still, the rupee's sharp losses in recent weeks have underlined how vulnerable the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is -- and raised questions about its future. While India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will take steps to curb further losses, analysts now increasingly believe that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  doesn't have much firepower left.\par \par \uc2 This means the rupee is likely headed lower.\par \par \uc2 "52 to 57 rupees can be the new short-term range," says Moses Harding, head of economic and market research at \uc2 IndusInd Bank Ltd\uc2 . in Mumbai. Indian companies that haven't started factoring in these high levels for the rupee in their business plans this year "will start doing it now," says Mr. Harding. At least one analyst thinks the rupee could fall to as much as 58 rupees for one U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Over the last week or so, the Indian rupee has been losing value primarily because global investors are becoming more risk averse as they worry about the fate of the Eurozone. "There's a desire to move back into the most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," meaning the U.S. dollar, says Dominic Bunning, associate \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Hong Kong. That has pushed up the value of the U.S. dollar against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While this has hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout Asia, the Indian rupee has had a steeper fall, largely because of a host of macro-economic problems specific to India. Since early March, the Indian rupee has dropped 9.3% against the U.S. dollar, whereas the South Korean won has lost just 4% against the greenback, says Mr. Bunning. His team at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  had a target of 54 rupees for one U.S. dollar by end June, but now believes it can fall further.\par \par \uc2 "We're still relatively bearish on the rupee," says Mr. Bunning.\par \par \uc2 This stems from factors including India's high current account deficit, lack of foreign capital flows, high inflation and failure to push through much-needed economic reforms.\par \par \uc2 And there is only so much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do to help the rupee. For instance, India's current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are enough to pay for six to seven months of Indian imports. Just a few years ago India's reserves could cover a year's worth of imports.\par \par \uc2 Ritika Mankar Mukherjee, an economist at Mumbai-based financial services firm Ambit Capital who has studied the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 2004, and found that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market alone didn't help the rupee much.\par \par \uc2 The balance of payments -- which reflects India's monetary transactions with the rest of the world -- is eight times a "more powerful driver" of rupee movement, Ms. Mukherjee said in a recent research report. She believes India's balance of payments is going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mukherjee projects that the pace of India's export growth is going to slow down by more than half in the financial year ending March 31, 2013. This is mainly because of the poor economic climate in Europe, a region that accounts for one-fifth of India's overall merchandise exports.\par \par \uc2 Another reason for a slowdown in export growth, says Ms. Mukherjee, is that it has been exaggerated in the past. In calendar year 2011 -- when export growth from major Asian countries like China, Japan, Singapore and Indonesia fell --India's exports grew at the same pace as they had a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 This doesn't sound right to her. "There is some amount of over-estimation there," says Ms. Mukherjee.\par \par \uc2 Over the year that started April 1, as India's exports growth comes off but imports stay high because of high oil and gold imports, Ms. Mukherjee expects India's current account deficit to widen further to 4.4%, versus 4% for the year that ended March 31. Overall, Ms. Mukherjee believes the rupee could go as low as 58 rupees for one U.S. dollar over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 "In the interim, there will clearly be pain," says Ms. Mukherjee.\par \par \uc2 You can follow Shefali and India Real Time on Twitter @shefalianand and @indiarealtime. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 17, 2012 02:23 ET (06:23 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120517e85h001ww\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
