\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: Did the RBI's Rate Cut Make a Difference?\b0\par\par\uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2012\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's India Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Shefali Anand\par \par \uc2 Many anticipated the cut in India's benchmark interest rate to be a panacea for India's weakening economy.\par \par \uc2 A month later, it has not yet had much of an impact: On some fronts, India's economy is in worse shape now than it was before the rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Data released on Monday showed that India's wholesale price index rose by 7.23% in April compared to the same period last year. Economists had expected inflation to rise by just 6.6%, partly because last April inflation had been especially high.\par \par \uc2 To tame India's uncomfortably high inflation rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised India's benchmark rate 13 times since March 2010 from 4.75% to 8.5%. On April 17, the RBI cut interest rates for the first time in three years by a higher-than-expected half a percentage point. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was under a lot of pressure from government officials, industrialists and investors.\par \par \uc2 Now, some analysts are questioning the timing and the size of the rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Taimur Baig, chief economist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank AG\uc2  in Singapore, says that in April the RBI was probably justified in cutting rates: inflation was rising at a slower pace and concerns over India's slowing economic growth were mounting.\par \par \uc2 However, "subsequent data now suggests that, that was probably not the right thing to do," Mr. Baig said in a phone interview.\par \par \uc2 A key reason is that India's inflation is driven less by a high demand for goods than by supply-side problems. Food items, for instance, often rot in warehouses because of poor food storage. "It's about structural issues," said Mr. Baig.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a sharp decline in the value of the Indian rupee in recent weeks has made India's imports even more costly, adding to inflationary pressures. The rupee has fallen 4% against the U.S. dollar since April 17, to trade at 53.80 rupees for one dollar on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, investment into India has slowed down partly as a result of the government's failure to push through policy reforms. Proposed tax changes, including plans to retroactively tax some transactions involving Indian assets, have also put off foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Minutes taken during a meeting in the run-up to the RBI's April monetary policy review show that an external advisory committee had advised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  against cutting the interest rate.\par \par \uc2 Four out of the six members of the Technical Advisory Committee "felt that unless supply side constraints were addressed and relevant measures were taken to revive investment activity, the reduction in the policy rate would not have any impact," said the minutes, which were released on Monday. The other two members suggested a rate cut of quarter percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Economists now worry that India faces the possibility of stagflation, in which inflation rises even as economic growth slows down.\par \par \uc2 Data released earlier this month showed that India's industrial output fell unexpectedly. Industrial output shrank 3.5% in March from a year earlier, compared with February's 4.1% expansion.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, some of this economic data is quite volatile and figures for one month don't provide an accurate reading of India's economic future. Still, these can impact perception among investors and businessmen.\par \par \uc2 In fact, stock market investors, who had been among the most vocal in demanding a rate cut, aren't too happy right now. The benchmark 30-share Sensex has fallen around 5% since April 17.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have pulled out around $10 million from Indian stocks since then.\par \par \uc2 This is partly because global investors have been fleeing risky assets in recent weeks. India's ailing domestic economy hasn't given them much of a reason to stay.\par \par \uc2 The large rate cut "was a bit overdone," said Paras Bothra, head of stock research at Kolkata-based securities firm Ashika Stock Broking Ltd.\par \par \uc2 "Imported inflation is going to be a big concern if the Indian rupee depreciates this way," he added.\par \par \uc2 To arrest the rupee's losses, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lately been more active in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, contrary to its previously stated stance of a hands-off policy toward the rupee.\par \par \uc2 It has also taken other steps, like forcing exporters to convert their dollar holdings. But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts don't believe these will be enough to boost the rupee, which is suffering because of macro-economic concerns, such as India's high trade deficit.\par \par \uc2 It's not all gloom for India's economy. Some economists, for instance, note that it's a good sign that price increases in India's manufacturing sector or so-called core inflation has slowed in April. Still, most believe that the RBI won't be cutting interest rates further in a hurry.\par \par \uc2 Over the next few months, we'll find out if the larger-than-expected rate cut in April was a blunder or a boon for India.\par \par \uc2 You can follow Shefali and India Real Time on Twitter @shefalianand and @indiarealtime. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 16, 2012 00:00 ET (04:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120516e85g000r0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
