\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Falls To New Record Low Against Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 196 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2012\par 08:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee tumbled to a new record low against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, tracking a slide in local shares as investors across the region dumped risk-sensitive assets amid the deepening political turmoil in Greece.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was quoted at INR54.26 at 0630 GMT after trading as high as INR54.30, surpassing its previous record high of INR54.2925 struck on Dec. 15.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's Sensitive Index was trading down 1.9% at 16,020.44 as global risk sentiment took a hit due to Greece's failure to form a coalition, raising fears that the country may exit the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee's recent losses come despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vigorous efforts to defend the unit. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. And in its most aggressive move so far, the RBI last week ordered exporters to convert half of their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding into rupees.\par \par \uc2 -By Sudeep Jain, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6123; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com [ 16-05-12 0635GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020120516e85g0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
