\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Indian Rupee Falls To Four-Month Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nupur Acharya and Shefali Anand  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2012\par 08:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee sunk to a four-month low against the U.S. dollar early Thursday amid growing concerns about the country's gaping budget and trade deficits and worries capital inflows will remain weak due to slowing domestic economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at 53.19 rupees in mid-morning trade. Earlier in the day, the dollar was up at 53.27 rupees, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lowest level in four months.\par \par \uc2 India's current account deficit, currently at about 4% of gross domestic product, has shaken investors, pushing \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  last month to warn that it may downgrade India's long-term debt to junk status if things don't improve.\par \par \uc2 The deficit, which reflects that imports are outpacing exports, has been fueled by higher oil import costs. The government's burgeoning budget deficit, caused by large subsidies on fuel and massive welfare program spending--coupled with slowing economic growth--has added to investor concern.\par \par \uc2 The economy grew 6.9% in the year to March 31, below rates of almost 8% in recent years amid signs foreign investors are turning their backs on India due to impatience over the slow pace of business reforms.\par \par \uc2 "A turnaround in the rupee is likely to be delayed if the recovery in India's growth disappoints or crude prices gain more than expected in the fiscal second half," Standard Chartered Bank said in a report.\par \par \uc2 For now, there're few signs of a turnaround in India's external trade picture. Data out earlier this week added to pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the government reported that India's merchandise exports fell 5.7% to $28.7 billion in March year-on-year. Imports grew 24.2% on-year to $42.6 billion, leading to a trade deficit in March of $13.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Higher imports mean India is buying more dollars, whereas lower exports reduce the supply of dollars in India.\par \par \uc2 Abhishek Goenka, chief executive officer of Mumbai-based India \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Advisors Pvt., said Indian importers were buying up dollars now expecting further weakness in the rupee in the near future, exacerbating pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "That sort of fear thing is coming in the market," Mr. Goenka said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has added to importers' fears of further weakness, he added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month cut its key interest rate by half a percentage point to 8%, its first cut in three years, in a bid to boost economic growth. But the bank also cautioned that it might not have room to cut rates much further in months ahead due to lingering concerns about inflation, which remains around 7%-8%.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts are now saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been too quick to loosen its monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 India relies on imports for 80% of its oil needs and a sharp rise in global crude prices could balloon the trade deficit and add to inflationary pressure later this year, a risk flagged in the HSBC Manufacturing PMI data for April released Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The seasonally adjusted \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Purchasing Managers' Index, prepared by Markit, rose to 54.9 in April from 54.7 in March, after slowing for two consecutive months.\par \par \uc2 Leif Eskesen, chief economist for India and Southeast Asia at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , said inflation accelerated with both output and input prices rising at a quicker pace in April.\par \par \uc2 "This suggests that upside risks to inflation remain and that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 's rate cut could turn out to have been premature and too aggressive," Eskesen said.\par \par \uc2 Kumar Rachapudi of \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said the fundamental picture for the rupee remains negative given the weak trade data. A poor global economic picture--with the deepening euro-zone debt crisis and a shaky recovery in the U.S.--have added to pressure on the rupee as investors look for U.S. dollar safe-haven investments.\par \par \uc2 "In the near term, there are pressures as (the) global backdrop remains weak and portfolio flows are muted, creating a pressure on the rupee," he said. "Further depreciation in the rupee will be due to a broader dollar strength and a sharp decline in the euro from the current levels," he added.\par \par \uc2 -By Nupur Acharya and Shefali Anand, The Wall Street Journal; Nupur.Acharya@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 03, 2012 02:37 ET (06:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120503e853001b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
