\par\par \b \uc2 S&P Outlook Revision Unlikely To Have Large Impact On Indian Rupee - Former RBI Panel Member\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sudeep Jain   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2012\par 14:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--\uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  move Wednesday to lower its outlook on India's long-term credit rating reflects the problems facing the economy, but is unlikely to have a large impact on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  panel member.\par \par \uc2 The action may make overseas debt costlier, but "the basic problem is not how to attract debt but investments," A.V. Rajwade told Dow Jones Newswires after S&P revised India's credit outlook to negative from stable while reaffirming its 'BBB-minus' rating.\par \par \uc2 Rajwade, a risk consultant whose views are closely watched in the market, has served on two \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  committees on capital-account convertibility.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee's volatility over the past eight months strengthens the case for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to actively manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent sharp movements, Rajwade said.\par \par \uc2 However, at present, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has little room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in heavy doses as buying the rupee could add to a cash crunch in the banking system.\par \par \uc2 India's shift to an almost fully floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few years has made the rupee vulnerable to recurring bouts of volatility, he said.\par \par \uc2 The comments come as the rupee has resumed its downward trend after gaining as much 9% against the U.S. dollar earlier this year. The dollar was last trading at INR52.54--close to the record high of INR54.2925 that the greenback hit in December.\par \par \uc2 Rajwade sees little respite for the rupee, given India's gaping current account deficit which the government expects to have widened to nearly 4% in the fiscal year ended March 31, the highest in 14 years.\par \par \uc2 India's dependence on imported coal and gas, apart from crude, is increasing and "I see very little prospects of an improvement in the trade or current accounts in 2012-13," he said.\par \par \uc2 India imports three-fourths of the crude oil it requires. The rising reliance on imports at a time of high commodity prices pushed the trade deficit to a record high of $185 billion in the fiscal year ended March 31.\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, capital inflows were sufficient to bridge India's current account gap, but global uncertainty and the absence of major financial reforms in a slowing economy are blocking fund inflows. In fact, so far in April, there has been a net portfolio capital outflow of $26.63 million.\par \par \uc2 -By Sudeep Jain, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6123; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com [ 25-04-12 1231GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120425e84p0011j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
