\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: India Adviser Forecasts Pressure On Rupee To Continue\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2012\par 14:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- PM's adviser expects rupee to stay around 52.50 to dollar\par \par \uc2 -- Expects muted capital flows, current account gap to weigh on rupee\par \par \uc2 -- Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may offer only temporary relief\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market Tuesday, dealers say\par \par \uc2 (Adds likely \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  level in 3rd-4th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 By Prasanta Sahu\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--An adviser to India's prime minister has cast doubt on the ability of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a swooning rupee, saying that muted foreign capital inflows and a wide current account gap will block any near-term rebound in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 M. Govinda Rao's comments come as the rupee Tuesday fell to a 15-week low against the dollar in early trade. The dollar rose to INR52.87, a level last seen on Jan. 6.\par \par \uc2 It pared some of the gains toward the close of trade in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , three dealers told Dow Jones Newswires. The RBI is likely to have sold dollars when the greenback was trading at INR52.78, they said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at INR52.68 late Tuesday in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the RBI may offer only a temporary relief, said Rao, a member of the Prime Minister's Economic Advisory Council.\par \par \uc2 "When you have such a large current account deficit and when the foreign fund flows are not adequate to meet the current account deficit," the rupee will hobble, Rao told Dow Jones Newswires late Monday.\par \par \uc2 He expects the rupee to trade around 52.50 to the dollar in the near term.\par \par \uc2 High crude oil prices and softening global demand for Indian goods are straining India's current account gap, which the government expects to have widened to nearly 4% in the fiscal year ended March 31, the highest in 14 years.\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, foreign capital inflows were adequate to fund India's current account gap. But a recent trend of capital outflows--this month alone, there has been a net foreign fund outflow of $24.5 million--has constrained the supply of dollars, increasing the downward pressure on the rupee.\par \par \uc2 Rao said the pressure on the rupee may ease if the government clarifies its stand on tax provisions, such as the General Anti-Avoidance Rule, which have hurt investor sentiment and fund flows.\par \par \uc2 The rule would empower tax authorities to scrutinize deals that they believe were structured solely to avoid taxes. The new rule, which was announced in the federal budget last month, is yet to get parliament clearance.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors fear the government may use it to demand tax arrears relating to investments made through tax havens such as Mauritius, with which India has a double-taxation avoidance treaty.\par \par \uc2 The government is likely to clarify its stand on the rule through amendments in the Finance Bill which will be put to vote in parliament next month.\par \par \uc2 -By Prasanta Sahu, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-11-43563311; anant.kala@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Sourav Mishra and Sudeep Jain in Mumbai contributed to this article [ 24-04-12 1252GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120424e84o000ym\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
