\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ India's Finance Minister Says No To More Rate Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2012\par 19:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:31 EDT *DJ  India Finance Minister: 'No Question Of Cutting' Rates Further For Now\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:31 EDT *DJ  India Finance Minister: Future Rate Moves Depends On Inflation\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:32 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister: No Need For Rupee \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Now\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:32 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister: Rupee Weak On Slower Exports, Trade Gap, Market Fluctuations\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:32 EDT *DJ  India Finance Minister: Indian Exports Hurt By Troubles in Europe\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:32 EDT *DJ  India Finance Minister: Foreign Investment Climate Remains Positive\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:33 EDT *DJ  India Finance Minister: Opening Insurance Sector To Higher Foreign Investment\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:33 EDT *DJ  India Finance Minister: BRICS Countries Decided To Disclose IMF Pledges Later\par \par \uc2 21 Apr 2012 13:37 EDT DJ  INTERVIEW: India's Finance Minister Says No To More Rate Cuts\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Nirmala Menon and Sudeep Reddy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--India's Finance Minister, Pranab Mukherjee, said it was "not possible" to cut interest rates further, despite calls by industry to slash further to boost domestic investment and kick-start economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate cut to 8% from 8.5% Tuesday was the first in three years and double the expected 25-basis-point reduction. The move was aimed at helping to spur growth in an economy hit by high credit costs and waning global demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Businesses had been complaining that tight monetary policy was hurting investment. Mukherjee's remarks suggest that policy makers believe one aggressive cut is enough to address the problem.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "No, there is no question of cutting further. Not now," Mukherjee said in an interview. Any future action by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would depend on whether inflationary pressure becomes an issue, he said--remarks that suggest the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only cut rates if inflation doesn't heat up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been one of the most hawkish in Asia in the last two years, raising its key lending rate 13 times between March 2010 and October 2011 to fight inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro-zone debt crisis has been a blow for India, which sends as much as 36% of its exports to Europe, according to Mukherjee. Europe is also a key source of foreign direct investment into India, which has been affected in the wake of the crisis. The Indian economy expanded 6.9% in the year to March 31, the slowest pace in three years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reduction comes amid fears that high crude oil prices, India's wide fiscal deficit, and a weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could again stoke inflation, which has slowed in recent months. The rupee was at about 52 to the dollar late Friday in New York, close to a record low above 54 hit in December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mukherjee said the Indian rupee has been weak because of "market fluctuation," weak exports and the country's trade deficit. Asked whether the government is prepared to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said "there is no need of (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) now."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Mukherjee sought to reassure investors in the wake of complaints about India's tax and investment climate by foreign companies. U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, in a meeting this week with the Indian finance minister, said recent tax provisions had "dampened enthusiasm about India's investment climate," a Treasury spokeswoman said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Proposals included in budget legislation that the Indian Parliament is expected to vote on in coming weeks would allow Indian tax authorities to impose taxes retrospectively on certain transactions involving foreign firms that date back to 1962.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mukherjee said the latest moves shouldn't hinder foreign companies. He said India has maintained policies that are "investment friendly and conducive to investment."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When a prospective investor wants to make an investment, he looks into the purchasing power of the people, whether the government's policy is stable, whether there is rule of law," Mukherjee said. India remains appealing "on all these counts," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While corruption scandals have drawn attention across India, "appropriate measures are being taken," Mukherjee said. "Therefore what is new in it? Sometimes it happens."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India would move ahead in opening up the insurance sector to higher foreign direct investment and also welcomed investment in the country's infrastructure, he said. The nation is trying to draw about $1 trillion in investment over the next five years in the transportation, communications, and power sectors. Approvals for foreign investment in most areas can be "cleared very easily," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mukherjee, who was in Washington for a meeting of the Group of 20 nations and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said India would contribute to the IMF's $430 billion fundraising drive but would disclose the size of its pledge later.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRICS nations--Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa--decided at a meeting this week to unveil their contributions once the countries had completed changes in their governments and internal consultations. Mukherjee said he would determine India's pledge after consulting with India's prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Nirmala Menon and Sudeep Reddy, Dow Jones Newswires; 613-761-2126; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 21, 2012 13:37 ET (17:37 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120421e84l000q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
