\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee logs 2nd straight weekly loss; outlook bearish\b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2012\par 04:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Domestic shares end 1.37 pct lower in choppy session\par \par \uc2 * Dollar demand from local oil refiners weigh on rupee\par \par \uc2 * Rupee at risk of weakening back to 53-54/dlr - \uc2 Citigroup\par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Aditya Phatak\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 13 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee logged its second straight weekly loss in the new fiscal year that began on April 1, giving up most of its intraday gains on Friday, dragged by a sharp fall in local shares and strong dollar demand from oil importers.\par \par \uc2 However, dollar sales by a few exporters and possibly some foreign banks for custodial flows offered some solace for the rupee, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The rupee ended at 51.3050 to the dollar, after hitting a high of 51.29 earlier. It closed at 51.580/585 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In the week, the rupee fell 0.5 percent taking its retreat from the 2012 peak of 48.60 to 5.3 percent, Thomson Reuters data showed.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar buying from oil companies was there, as was the fall in euro and stocks to pressure rupee," said Ashtosh Raina, head of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 India imports nearly 80 percent of its crude oil needs, making local oil firms the biggest buyers of dollars in the domestic foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "But there were some dollar inflows that helped deflect some of the selling pressure on rupee," Raina said.\par \par \uc2 India's main share index fell 1.37 percent, led by \uc2 Infosys Ltd\uc2  after its guidance of 8-10 growth in dollar revenue for the next fiscal year missed the market's estimates. A fall in European shares on lower-than-expected Chinese growth data also dampened mood.\par \par \uc2 Until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy review next Tuesday, most traders expect the rupee to be stuck in a 50.80 to 51.80 range. They expect the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in case of a sharp drop in the rupee.\par \par \uc2 The RBI, which is due to announce its policy statement for 2012/13 on Tuesday, is widely expected to cut interest rates to support the flagging economy.\par \par \uc2 Traders believe the RBI rate action and statement could also provide some clues about capital flows, which have come under a cloud due to proposed tax changes by the government.\par \par \uc2 But some predict a weak patch for the rupee in coming days given the fragile global growth and weak Indian macro-economic indicators such as widening trade and current account deficits along with high inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Quality of capital flows has been deteriorating with increasing reliance on non-FDI flows to fund current account deficit. The USD/INR is at the risk of returning to 53-54," said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in a note.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had touched a record low of 54.30 in mid-December 2011 and fallen nearly 16 percent during that year.\par \par \uc2 The one-month offshore non-deliverable forward contracts\par \par \uc2 were at 51.65.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market, the most-traded near-month dollar-rupee contracts on the National Stock Exchange, the MCX-SX and on the United Stock Exchange all ended at around 51.47, on a total volume of $3.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Malini Menon)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA/RUPEE (UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | SNI | SNS | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120416e84d00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
