\par\par \b \uc2 India  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Adviser: Rupee At 50.00 To A Dollar Reflects Current Economic Fundamentals\b0\par\par\uc2 151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 10:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ensure that the rupee doesn't weaken significantly beyond its fair value of 50.00 to a U.S. dollar, a senior adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 While the RBI's scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited by the moderate size of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, its mere presence in the market sends an adequate signal, Ashima Goyal, a member of the RBI's technical advisory committee on monetary policy, told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The comments come at a time when the rupee is trading at a more than two-month low of 51.09 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 --By Khushita Vasant, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6123; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com [ 22-03-12 1021GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020120322e83m00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
