\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: India  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Acknowledges Govt's Efforts To Lower Fiscal Gap\b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 15:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Budget presents significant fiscal consolidation, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official says\par \par \uc2 -- New revenue proposals increase reliability of fiscal consolidation process\par \par \uc2 -- Liquidity conditions to determine further bond buybacks\par \par \uc2 (Combines related articles, adds deputy governors' comments in 4th, 10th, 14th, 16th and 17th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 By Nupur Acharya and Sudeep Jain\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday applauded the government's plan to lower the fiscal deficit but said it will also consider crude oil prices and economic growth while deciding on interest-rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The comments are important because the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  had recently stepped up its criticism of the government's lax fiscal policies, which are seen as diluting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to curb inflation through a tight monetary policy. The RBI has said that it would be constrained from lowering rates in the absence of credible fiscal consolidation.\par \par \uc2 The RBI left its lending rate unchanged at 8.50% Thursday, saying the risks to inflation had increased due to higher crude prices, the large fiscal gap and a weakening rupee. The RBI next meets to review monetary policy April 17.\par \par \uc2 "A significant part of the correction [in the fiscal deficit] is coming from new revenue proposals, particularly indirect tax proposals, so... the reliability of the process goes up," RBI Deputy Governor Subir Gokarn said at a press conference.\par \par \uc2 India aims to reduce its fiscal deficit to 5.1% of gross domestic product in the next fiscal year that starts April from 5.9% in the current year, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee told parliament in his budget speech Friday.\par \par \uc2 The government is banking on lower subsidies, an economic recovery and an increase in indirect taxes to meet the deficit target.\par \par \uc2 The government will attempt to keep subsidies below 2% of GDP next fiscal year and try to keep it below 1.7% over the following three years, Mukherjee said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can draw comfort from the government's efforts to reduce its fiscal deficit, which appear credible, Ashima Goyal, a member of the RBI's technical advisory committee on monetary policy, told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Sudipto Mundle, another member of the committee, said the budget targets are realistic and would help reinforce credibility of the government's projections on the fiscal deficit.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Governor Gokarn also said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would decide whether to carry out more bond buybacks depending on the liquidity conditions.\par \par \uc2 The banking system has been facing an acute liquidity shortage due to the government's over-the-top borrowing program, advance tax payments by companies and the RBI's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to protect the rupee.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $1.030 billion to $293.959 billion in the week ended March 9, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed Friday.\par \par \uc2 The RBI has bought back government bonds on a large scale and has slashed banks' minimum cash requirement by 125 basis points in less than two months to pump cash into the system.\par \par \uc2 Separately, RBI Deputy Governor H.R. Khan said it would be difficult to manage the government's huge borrowing program.\par \par \uc2 The government plans to borrow a net INR4.79 trillion from the market next fiscal year to fund its budget, compared with INR4.4 trillion this year.\par \par \uc2 Officials from the RBI and India's finance ministry are likely to meet on March 27 or March 28 to decide on the government's borrowing calendar for the first half of the next fiscal year, Khan said.\par \par \uc2 Khan also said that he expects INR600 billion to flow back into the Indian banking system in the first fortnight of April as the government spends or refunds advance tax payments of companies.\par \par \uc2 - By Sudeep Jain, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6145 6123; sudeep.jain@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Anant Vijay Kala in New Delhi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 16, 2012 10:23 ET (14:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120316e83g00492\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
