\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slides to 7-wk low on importers\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 03:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Gold, oil importers step up dollar purchases\par \par \uc2 * Worries over global econ outlook weigh on rupee\par \par \uc2 * Expectations of RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen aiding rupee\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, comments, updates to mid-morning)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 7 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee continued to weaken for the fifth consecutive session on Wednesday, sliding to a seven-week low, due to incessant dollar demand from oil refiners and gold importers.\par \par \uc2 Mounting worries over slowing world economies and renewed uncertainty over Greece's bailout added to the downward pressure on the rupee, traders said.\par \par \uc2 At 11:01 a.m. (0531 GMT), the rupee was at 50.64/65 to the dollar, after dipping to 50.76, its lowest since Jan. 18, according to Thomson Reuters data. The unit had closed at 50.36/37 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There is huge demand in the market. It seems that everybody just wants to buy dollars including most sections of importers," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with a large state-run bank.\par \par \uc2 Local oil refiners have been buying dollars aggressively in the past few sessions due to a recent surge in global oil prices, with India importing nearly 80 percent of its oil needs.\par \par \uc2 Demand for gold in India, the world's largest consumer of the bullion, has climbed amid the festival and wedding season after prices fell 2.8 percent since Feb. 27.\par \par \uc2 Outlook for foreign inflows has been clouded as expectations of a revival in reforms were jolted after the Congress party's severe losses in crucial state elections on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 However, expectations that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take action and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the rupee is likely to provide some support to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 "People do remember what happened last time when they tested RBI's resolve so they will be even more careful now. You don't want the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shut you from the market completely," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a private sector bank.\par \par \uc2 The RBI has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past few months to support the rupee, which touched a record low of 54.30 on Dec. 15, and took numerous steps including slashing of banks' trading limits to cut speculative trades.\par \par \uc2 The one-month offshore non-deliverable forward contracts\par \par \uc2 were at 51.04.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market, the most-traded near-month dollar-rupee contracts on the National Stock Exchange, the MCX-SX and the United Stock Exchange were all around 50.89, on a total volume of $1.92 billion.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Aditya Phatak; Editing by Aradhana Aravindan)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA/RUPEE (UPDATE 1) | ABN | E | RBN | M | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120310e83700017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
