\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EXCLUSIVE-Iran defaults on rice payments to India-traders\b0\par\par\uc2 1,361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2012\par 03:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story with no changes to text)\par \par \uc2 * Payment defaults on about 200,000 tonnes of rice\par \par \uc2 * Indian rice exporters fear further payment defaults\par \par \uc2 * Exporters' group asks government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets about 70 pct of Iran rice needs\par \par \uc2 By Ratnajyoti Dutta and Mayank Bhardwaj\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Feb 7 (Reuters) - Iranian buyers have defaulted on payments for about 200,000 tonnes of rice from their top supplier India, exporters and rice millers said on Tuesday, a sign of the mounting pressure on Tehran from a new wave of Western sanctions.\par \par \uc2 The default prompted the head of the All India Rice Exporters' Association to call on members to stop rice exports to Iran based on credit, which would be a fresh blow to a country where imports of staple foods are already being hampered by sanctions.\par \par \uc2 "It is a serious issue and we do not rule out further payment defaults by Iran," said Vijay Setia, the association's president.\par \par \uc2 Indian sources said the Iranian buyers had defaulted on payments worth about $144 million for rice shipments under long-term supply deals. Iran shipped the cargoes from Indian ports in October and November. Most Indian rice exporters allow 90 days credit.\par \par \uc2 Iran relies on imports for about 45 pct of its annual rice consumption of 2.9 million tonnes, according to U.S. Dept. of Agriculture data.\par \par \uc2 In other supply disruptions, five deliveries of grain to Iran were diverted to new destinations because payments were held up, ship tracking data showed last week. Other cargoes are sitting offshore Iran because of difficulty with payments.\par \par \uc2 Under a tightening grip of sanctions, the country of 74 million people is finding it increasingly difficult to repatriate the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its crude oil exports, its major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earner, that it needs to pay for shipments of food and other imports.\par \par \uc2 A sharp drop in the value of the rial is adding to Iran's import costs and the financial sanctions make it difficult for traders in the country to channel import payments through unofficial routes involving middlemen based in Dubai.\par \par \uc2 India is Iran's top rice supplier, accounting for some 70 percent of its annual requirement of 1-1.2 million tonnes of the grain, mainly the aromatic variety called Basmati.\par \par \uc2 Traders and officials in Iran could not be immediately reached to comment.\par \par \uc2 The United States slapped fresh sanctions on Tehran from the start of this year, targeting financial institutions that deal with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hoping to stem oil revenues and persuade Iran to abandon a suspected nuclear weapons programme.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President Barack Obama tightened those sanctions on Sunday, again targeting Iran's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and giving U.S. banks new powers to freeze assets linked to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 The European Union has agreed to ban Iranian oil imports, a measure expected to take full effect within six months.\par \par \uc2 SANCTIONS BITING?\par \par \uc2 The rice defaults could be the latest sign that those sanctions are biting as Iranian importers find it increasingly difficult to settle payments.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's maize exports to Iran dropped 40 percent in January due to payment problems, Ukrainian consultancy ProAgro said last week.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian and European traders said they were no longer booking Ukraine grains shipments to Iran because of the payments difficulties, although talks were underway to save the market, the country's farm minister said.\par \par \uc2 Five ships of grain have been diverted away from Iran to other destinations and about 400,000 tonnes of grains on at least 10 vessels have been held up outside Iranian ports because of payments problems, trade sources said last week.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rice association's Setia said the group had approached the Indian government to recover their dues.\par \par \uc2 "As part of efforts to minimise losses, we are asking our colleagues to avoid sending rice on credit," he said, adding they were not calling for a ban on exports to Iran.\par \par \uc2 A government source acknowledged exporters had sought help.\par \par \uc2 The rice default is the latest snag in India-Iran trade, which is heavily skewed towards Tehran.\par \par \uc2 India is Iran's second-largest buyer of crude and it has struggled to settle payments worth some $11 billion annually after New Delhi scrapped a long-standing mechanism in 2010 under pressure from Washington.\par \par \uc2 While New Delhi has switched to a payment conduit for its Iranian oil using a Turkish bank, Indian rice exporters have been using a loose, unofficial route involving a network of middlemen based in Dubai.\par \par \uc2 The middlemen receive payments from Iranian importers in rial and pay Indian exporters in U.S. dollars. The consignments are sent directly from India to Iran.\par \par \uc2 But Indian traders said the defaults started after the rial plunged over the past month, making previous purchases costlier for Iranian importers.\par \par \uc2 The cost of transactions also went up for the Dubai middlemen as they use U.S. dollars to pay Indian exporters.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 26, Iran announced an 8 percent devaluation of the rial and said it would enforce a single exchange rate, aiming to stamp out a black market where the dollar's value has soared due to fears over new sanctions imposed by the West.\par \par \uc2 Indian traders said about 20 Iranian companies have failed to clear their dues during the past month.\par \par \uc2 "It threatens to jeopardise the trade with them, hurting both India and Iran," Anil K. Mittal, chairman of KRBL Ltd, a leading Indian rice miller and exporter, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "Due to sanctions on Iran, currently banks are not involved in payments to Indian rice suppliers. The payments are direct and at times on credit, making Indian exporters vulnerable to defaults. Indian traders must avoid supplying on credit."\par \par \uc2 Setia said India should not send any more rice to Iran on credit, adding suppliers such as those in Thailand, Vietnam and Pakistan had already stopped doing so.\par \par \uc2 However, the Thai Rice Exporters' Association said Thailand had stopped selling rice to Iran about a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Javed Agha, chairman of the Rice Exporters Association of Pakistan acknowledged that sanctions had made traders reluctant in both Pakistan and Iran to do deals.\par \par \uc2 "Banking if a problem between Pakistan and Iran," he said. "There is no proper system."\par \par \uc2 BOOSTING EXPORTS TO IRAN\par \par \uc2 New Delhi will send a delegation to Iran this month to explore boosting exports to smooth use of the restricted rupee \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the two sides have agreed to use to settle 45 percent of India's $11 billion a year oil bill.\par \par \uc2 But this would give Iran large amounts of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which is difficult to use for international trade.\par \par \uc2 An Indian ministerial panel on food exports meets on Tuesday to review rice and sugar shipments. The issue of exports to Iran is not on the agenda, government officials said, seeking to knock down reports that the panel could discuss the possibility of boosting rice and wheat exports to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 India, is expected to produce 102.75 million tonnes of rice in the crop year to June 2012, a farm ministry forecast said last month. Basmati makes up around 5 percent of India's total rice production.\par \par \uc2 India's Jan. 1 rice inventory at government warehouses was 29.8 million tonnes against a target of 11.8 million tonnes.\par \par \uc2 China, Japan, India and South Korea, which together buy about half or Iran's 2.6 million barrels per day of crude exports, are under pressure from U.S. sanctions to cut back their purchases.\par \par \uc2 China has already cut Iran imports in the first three months of this year over a pricing dispute and a flurry of U.S. diplomats have discussed the sanctions with Asian government officials.\par \par \uc2 South Korea is paying Iran for crude in its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but difficulty repatriating the funds under the weight of sanctions means won worth an estimated $5 billion has piled up South Korean banks.\par \par \uc2 ($1=48.93 rupees)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Qasim Nauman in Islamabad and [20120208 020335 GMT]\par \par \uc2 Apornrath Phoonphongphiphat in Bangkok; Writing by Krittivas Mukherjee; Editing by John Chalmers and Neil Fullick) [20120208 020336 GMT]\par \par \uc2 INDIA-RICE/ (UPDATE 1, EXCLUSIVE)  (REPEAT)|INDIA-RICE/ (UPDATE 1, EXCLUSIVE)  (REPEAT)=2|chained   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120208e8280002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
