\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Japan, India Agree To Set Up $15 Billion Swap Line\b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2011\par 16:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -The swap line is five times the countries' previous arrangement\par \par \uc2 -It can help buttress the two economies against euro-zone debt crisis\par \par \uc2 -The swap line could help the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support the weak rupee\par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst's comment in 6th paragraph, context about rupee in 5th, 7th-8th paragraphs.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Prasanta Sahu \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW DELHI (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--The Japanese and Indian governments agreed Wednesday to set up a three-year, $15 billion bilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap line in an effort to buttress their economies against Europe's sovereign debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new swap line--five times the previous arrangement, which expired in early summer--follows a Japan-South Korea deal in October to boost their bilateral swap pact to $70 billion from $13 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The moves signal spreading doubts among Asian economic powerhouses about the ability of European leaders to fix their problems anytime soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At a time when the world economy is unstable, as shown by Europe's sovereign problems and other issues, Japan and India both recognized the need to bolster a Japan-India \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap line to ensure stability in the financial markets," Japan's Finance Ministry said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The swap agreement could help the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  support the Indian rupee, which has fallen nearly 15% against the U.S. dollar since April, making it the worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It will backstop the Indian rupee and reduce the pessimism," said Priyanka Kishore, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI has frequently said it doesn't want to deplete \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves through regular \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to steady the rupee, but would only step in to check excess volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI has so far stopped short of aggressively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, but had taken a number of small measures to boost capital inflows and curb speculation in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The swap deal is part of a package of agreements that includes Indian pledges to relax financial regulation so that Japanese companies can make investments more easily.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns have been growing about the damage Europe's debt problems could do to Asia, one of the world's most important growth drivers, if, for example, European banks unwind their investments in Asia to cover losses from their holdings of risky European government debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan surprised the world over the weekend by announcing a broad set of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deals with China, the world's second-largest economy after the U.S., but Tokyo sees India as key to its economic future. India's high growth rate, driven by local consumption, offers attractive export prospects. Over the three years through 2010, Japan's exports to India rose 47% to $9 billion, while its imports from the South Asian country climbed 36% to $5.7 billion, the Japanese finance ministry said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi & Prasanta Sahu; Dow Jones Newswires; 91-11-43563301; abhrajit.gangopadhyay@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 28, 2011 10:51 ET (15:51 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111228e7cs003bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
